  <header class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">The house</span>
    <h1>The History of the House</h1>
    <p>
      One of the oldest houses in Independence County, built in 1847.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>In the family's words</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">
      The following history was written by Robert Desha Stroud, a direct descendant, and is reproduced here as he wrote it.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="family-account">
      <p>Franklin W. Desha was born in Washington, D. C. February 27, 1819.   His father, Robert Montgomery Desha was in the Marine Corps at the time and was later appointed Quartermaster of the Mississippi region of the Marines.  When Franklin W. Desha was two years old and living with his parents in Helena, Arkansas, his father died of yellow fever while in New Orleans.  Robert’s father was Joseph Desha, Governor of Kentucky from 1824 to 1828.</p>
      <p>One of Robert’s sisters, Rachel Harriet Desha, had married Hartwell Boswell.  After Rachel died, Boswell married Frances Ann Ferebee Desha, the widowed mother of F. W. Desha, and they moved to Batesville.  Hartwell Boswell was in charge of public lands for the northern part of the territory of Arkansas.  He died in Batesville in 1834.</p>
      <p>Upon reaching the age of about sixteen, F. W. went to Kentucky to attend Centre College and then graduated from the law and literary department of Transylvania University, at Lexington, Ky.  In 1845 he was admitted to the bar of Arkansas and practiced in Batesville.</p>
      <p>At the outbreak of war between this country and Mexico, he enlisted in Company D, First Arkansas Mounted Infantry, in which he held the rank of lieutenant. On the occasion of the captain’s death, at the battle of Buena Vista, Lieut. Desha was promoted to fill his place.</p>
      <p>In 1847 he returned to Batesville and married Elizabeth Jett Searcy Desha.  She was the daughter of James and Martha Searcy, of Virginia, who were married in Alabama, and had moved to Batesville, Ark., in 1829, being then among the first settlers of that county. Mr. Searcy was of Scotch descent and served under General Andrew Jackson in the War of 1812. James Searcy afterward became a prominent farmer in Independence County, where he resided until his death in 1835, his wife following him the same year.  Richard Searcy, brother of James, was a prominent citizen in the area and reportedly the first Judge in Arkansas.</p>
      <p>F. W. and Elizabeth built their home in 1847 south of Batesville on the bluff overlooking the White River and farmland. He was a prosecutor of his district at the outbreak of war and as such was a delegate to the Secession Convention where he voted with the majority against secession.  The second Secession Convention, which occurred after Fort Sumter, declared the State of Arkansas out of the Union.  That time F. W. voted with the majority to secede.  He again enlisted and organized what was known as Desha’s Battalion, which he commanded until after the Battle of Shiloh, when he resigned on account of ill health, and returned to his home. Mr. Desha was at one time a member of Capt. Rutherford’s company in Price’s raids through Missouri and reportedly was wounded at the Battle of Fitzhugh’s Woods in Woodruff County, Arkansas, April 1, 1864.  He died at his home in 1869.</p>
      <p>Of the eleven children born to F. W. and Elizabeth, only five survived infancy.  Robert Desha, the oldest, owned about 700 acres of land, and had some 250 acres under cultivation in valuable bottomland.  In politics he was a strong Democrat, and from 1882 to 1886 he was Sheriff of Independence County.  He had two brothers, Stonewall Jackson Desha and Ben Desha, and two sisters, Mary Jane McGuire Desha and Elizabeth Jett Desha.  Stonewall made his way to Texas and died there, and Ben Desha became one of Independence County’s largest landowners and entrepreneurs, owing a ferry, a cotton gin, and many acres of farmland.  None of the brothers married or had children. Mary Jane lived unmarried with her parents until her death.  All eleven children of F. W. and Elizabeth Desha are buried at the family cemetery near their home.</p>
      <p>Elizabeth Jett Desha married W. H. Lester, and they parented the only descendant of that generation of the local Desha family.  Their son, Desha Lester, married Pearl Craig of Newark.  Their only child, Betty Lee Lester, married Robert Stroud, and they were the parents of Robert Desha Stroud and Mary Carolyn Stroud Vandiver.</p>
      <p>The Desha home known to many as “Fox Hill” remains in the family and is on the National Registry of Historical Places.</p>
      <footer class="family-account-source">— Robert Desha Stroud</footer>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The encampment of 1863</h2>
    <article class="prose">
      <p>
        In 1863, during the Civil War, the property was the site of a Confederate encampment. A historical
        marker near the city of Desha marks the encampment. Franklin Desha's own war service, including
        Desha's Battalion and his wound at Fitzhugh's Woods, is described in the family's account above.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The architecture</h2>
    <article class="prose">
      <p>
        The Franklin Desha House is a <strong>single-story double-pen dogtrot</strong>. It has two
        rooms, called pens, with an open breezeway between them under the same roof. The breezeway
        let air move through the house in summer, and it gave the family covered space for work and
        storage when the weather was bad.
      </p>
      <p>
        The main roof is a side gable. At the center of the front, a smaller gabled section projects
        over the entry between the two pens. Settlers from the Upper South built houses like this as
        they moved west. The Desha family made the same move, from Kentucky to Arkansas.
      </p>
